From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Doll <brektyme@brektyme.com>
Date: Tue, 23 Sep 2026 10:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: Gate ICM per controller on NVM_AUTH_DONE on Apple
 T2 Ice Lake

The Ice Lake entry in icm_probe() uses icm_fr_is_supported(), a blanket
"never on Apple". On a MacBookPro16,2 that is wrong for whichever of the
two NHIs holds a firmware-established tunnel at boot.

Apple's firmware brings Thunderbolt up before Linux, so a device attached
at power-on is already tunneled. On the controller carrying that tunnel
the T2's ICM is running and owns the control channel, and the software
connection manager cannot reach the router at all:

  thunderbolt 0000:00:0d.2: error -ETIMEDOUT: failed to allocate host router
  thunderbolt 0000:00:0d.2: probe with driver thunderbolt failed with error -110

That is TB_CTL_RETRIES (4) x TB_TIMEOUT (100 ms) of silence on the first
tb_cfg_read() of the root router. The failure follows the attached device
between controllers, and a module load long after boot fails identically,
so it is not a probe-ordering race.

The idle NHI must keep the software connection manager: with no ICM
running it never answers ICM driver ready, and enabling ICM on both
controllers costs ICM_RETRIES x 20 s before each domain is torn down.

icm_firmware_running() cannot be used to tell them apart. It reads
ICM_EN, which the driver itself sets while initialising the NHI, so by
the time is_supported() runs it is set on both -- on the idle controller
REG_FW_STS reads 0x00000100 before the driver loads and 0x000001a1 after
a successful probe. NVM_AUTH_DONE is set only by the firmware, and only
on the controller it drives:

  0000:00:0d.2 holding a tunnel  REG_FW_STS 0x800001a1  NVM_AUTH_DONE 1
  0000:00:0d.3 idle              REG_FW_STS 0x00000100  NVM_AUTH_DONE 0

both read with thunderbolt blacklisted, before the driver had touched
either controller.

Give Ice Lake its own predicate that keeps the unconditional "yes" for
non-Apple systems and otherwise tests NVM_AUTH_DONE, as
icm_tgl_is_supported() already does for later generations.

Tested on MacBookPro16,2 with an OWC Thunderbolt 3 Dock attached at power
on to either controller: the one holding the tunnel comes up on the
firmware connection manager and enumerates the device, the idle one uses
the software connection manager as before. Also tested with no device
attached, on hotplug after boot, and across suspend/resume.

Signed-off-by: Joshua Doll <brektyme@brektyme.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/icm.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/icm.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/icm.c
@@ -1657,6 +1657,25 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static bool icm_icl_is_supported(struct tb *tb)
+{
+	if (!x86_apple_machine)
+		return true;
+
+	/*
+	 * On Apple T2 the ICM only runs on the controller whose ports the
+	 * firmware has already tunneled; the idle one never answers driver
+	 * ready and costs ICM_RETRIES * 20 s before the domain is torn down.
+	 *
+	 * ICM_EN cannot tell them apart: it reads back set on both once the
+	 * driver has initialised the NHI, and clear on both before that.
+	 * NVM_AUTH_DONE is only ever set by the firmware, and only on the
+	 * controller it is actually driving.
+	 */
+	return !!(ioread32(tb->nhi->iobase + REG_FW_STS) &
+		  REG_FW_STS_NVM_AUTH_DONE);
+}
+
 static int
 icm_icl_driver_ready(struct tb *tb, enum tb_security_level *security_level,
 		     u8 *proto_version, size_t *nboot_acl, bool *rpm)
@@ -2548,7 +2567,7 @@
 
 	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI0:
 	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI1:
-		icm->is_supported = icm_fr_is_supported;
+		icm->is_supported = icm_icl_is_supported;
 		icm->driver_ready = icm_icl_driver_ready;
 		icm->set_uuid = icm_icl_set_uuid;
 		icm->device_connected = icm_icl_device_connected;
